<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#dong-son-culture">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Đông Sơn culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000 BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Dong Son culture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Đông Sơn culture was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture of the Red River and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-river">Ma River</a> valleys of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, named after a village in Thanh Hóa province. It is best known for large decorated bronze drums, traded widely across South-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Historians associate it with the early polities of Văn Lang and Âu Lạc.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture.jpg" alt="Đông Sơn culture" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bình Giang · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dong_Son_axe.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars traditionally traced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-casting">bronze-casting</a> technology to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, but discoveries of the 1970s at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Isan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> suggested that casting either began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> first and spread into China, or developed there independently. The Đông Sơn bronze industry therefore has a local origin in Southeast Asia and was not brought by migrations out of China. The Gò Mun culture gave rise to the Đông Sơn culture, which was the culmination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the opening stage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Objects related to Đông Sơn have been found in Malaysia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and the Chinese provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Đông Sơn people cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, kept water buffalo and pigs, fished and sailed in long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a>. They were also skilled bronze casters. Their Heger I drums are decorated with rings of warships and warriors in feather headdresses circling a central sun, on which stylised frogs rest; the scenes also show drummers and other musicians, rice processing, birds, deer and geometric designs. The chief summoned the warriors of the tribe by beating the drum, and drums were also used in mourning and at feasts. The Cổ Loa drum weighs 72 kilograms and would have needed between 1 and 7 tonnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ore. Some Đông Sơn daggers closely resemble Scytho-Siberian styles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Excavations at Đông Sơn village began in 1924 after a fisherman, Nguyễn Văn Lắm, reported large quantities of bronze objects to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-francaise-dextreme-orient">École française d'Extrême-Orient</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. The Austrian archaeologist Franz Heger had classified bronze drums in 1898. Scholars disagree about who the Dongsonians were. Ferlus concluded from vocabulary for the pestle, oar and sticky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> pan that they were of Vietic origin and direct ancestors of the Vietnamese, while Keith W. Taylor, Nguyen Phuong and John D. Phan hold there is no evidence they were Vietnamese or their ancestors.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-drum">Đông Sơn drums</a> vanish from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> after the defeat of the Trưng sisters and the arrival of Han material culture, after the Han general Ma Yuan conquered the Red River Plain. More than 120 Han brick tombs in the region point to growing Chinese migration or cultural influence, and the earliest Sino-Vietnamese loanwords date from about then.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đông Sơn culture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom rested on rice farming and <b>Dong Son</b> bronze working, and the Lac lords of the older order seem to have kept local power under the new king.</blockquote><a href="/state/au-lac">Au Lac</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians link it instead to the <b>Dong Son culture</b> of the Red River and Ma River valleys, which flourished from about the 7th century BCE, with its centre at Phong Chau.</blockquote><a href="/state/van-lang">Van Lang</a></li></ul></section>
